<MudDialog>
    <TitleContent>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">How to play</MudText>
    </TitleContent>
    <DialogContent>
        <div class="help-guide" data-testid="game-help-guide">
            <p>Manage a classic team one decision at a time. <strong>Pitch</strong> resolves the current plate appearance, or a steal attempt when Steal is selected. The computer handles any team you do not manage.</p>

            <h3>Start a game</h3>
            <p>Choose the visiting and home teams, then choose which side you manage. Open <strong>Game options</strong> to change the ballpark, starting pitchers, field view, random events, or starting lineups. A saved game can be resumed from the setup screen.</p>

            <h3>Offense</h3>
            <dl>
                <dt>Swing Away</dt><dd>Use the standard at-bat and base-running rules.</dd>
                <dt>Aggressive</dt><dd>Send runners for extra bases more often on hits. A runner on first can try to score on a double. A runner who does not make it is usually thrown out, so it pays with fast runners and a weak outfield arm.</dd>
                <dt>Steal</dt><dd>Send a runner before the batter's turn. With a runner on first and second open he goes for second; with a runner on second and third open he goes for third (harder, and rarely worth it); with runners on first and second and third open it is a double steal, where the catcher picks a base to throw to and the other runner is usually safe. The attempt can succeed or end in a caught stealing.</dd>
                <dt>Hit &amp; Run</dt><dd>The runner on first goes with the pitch (it needs a runner on first and second open). The batter strikes out less and a grounder is much less likely to be a double play, and the runner takes the extra base more often on a hit. The risks: a strikeout can also be a runner thrown out at second, and a line drive can double him off first. A pitchout beats it.</dd>
                <dt>Sacrifice</dt><dd>The batter bunts. With a runner on and fewer than two out it is a sacrifice: the batter is usually thrown out and the runners move up a base, and a runner on third scores. The defense can take the lead runner instead (fielder's choice), the ball can be popped up, and the fielder can boot it. With the bases empty, or with two out, it is a bunt for a hit: it rarely works, but a fast batter can beat it out. Corners In is the defense for it.</dd>
                <dt>Squeeze</dt><dd>With a runner on third and fewer than two out, the runner breaks with the pitch and the batter bunts. The run usually scores, but a bunt that is missed or popped up doubles the runner off third, and a defense that reads it can throw him out at the plate (the batter is then safe at first). Corners In makes both likelier.</dd>
                <dt>Safe squeeze</dt><dd>The runner on third waits to see the bunt on the ground before he goes. It never costs him on a popped-up bunt, but the run scores only some of the time (a fast runner and a good bunter help); otherwise he holds at third and the batter is out.</dd>
                <dt>Designated hitter (Game options)</dt><dd>Choose a designated hitter for both teams, the home team's league and year (no designated hitter before 1973 in the American League, or before 2022 in the National League), or pitchers bat. When the pitcher bats he is ninth in the order and can bunt. A pinch hitter for him sends him out of the game and a reliever takes over when you next take the field; the new pitcher takes the old one's place in the order; and the Subs dialog's Pitching tab offers a double switch.</dd>
                <dt>Safe</dt><dd>Selectable, but currently resolves with the standard at-bat rules; it does not yet have a separate simulation effect.</dd>
            </dl>

            <h3>Defense</h3>
            <dl>
                <dt>Fastball</dt><dd>Raises strikeout and home-run chances while reducing walks.</dd>
                <dt>Curveball</dt><dd>Lowers home-run chances, with more walks.</dd>
                <dt>Slider</dt><dd>Raises strikeout chances, with slightly more walks.</dd>
                <dt>Changeup</dt><dd>Lowers home-run and strikeout chances.</dd>
                <dt>Normal, In, Corners, Lines</dt><dd>Normal is the baseline, and none of the others beats it against an ordinary hitter with the bases empty. In brings the infield in so a runner on third usually has to hold on a ground ball (unless he is forced), at the cost of more singles getting through. Corners charge a bunt; against a full swing more balls get past them. Lines guards the foul lines: fewer doubles and triples, more singles, which pays late with a runner on first.</dd>
                <dt>Shift Pull, Shift Oppo</dt><dd>The defense moves toward the side the batter pulls (Pull), which opens more opposite-field singles but fewer doubles, or away from it (Oppo), which cuts singles but gives up more doubles. Which way that is depends on the batter's hand, and the fielders move when the batter changes.</dd>
                <dt>IBB</dt><dd>Issue an intentional walk instead of pitching to the batter. Forced runners advance.</dd>
            </dl>

            <h3>Manage the roster</h3>
            <p><strong>Subs</strong> opens pinch hitting, pinch running, fielder replacement, position swaps, and pitching changes. Warm a reliever for 4–10 batters faced before bringing him in; entering cold or staying warm too long reduces his early effectiveness. A removed player cannot return. Batters hit pitchers of the other hand better, a left-handed batter against a left-hander least of all, so a matchup is a reason to pinch hit or change pitchers.</p>

            <h3>Follow the game</h3>
            <p>The scoreboard shows the score and inning; the diamond shows runners and outs. On phones, a compact score appears after the full scoreboard scrolls away. The play-by-play log records completed plays. <strong>Box score</strong> shows batting and pitching totals, and <strong>Recap</strong> summarizes the game.</p>
            <p>Switch between <strong>Classic</strong> overhead and <strong>Detailed</strong> animated field views during play. Detailed view has animation speed controls and a Skip button. The Cast button sends the field to a compatible TV; the sound button mutes or unmutes audio.</p>

            <h3>Keyboard</h3>
            <p>Press <strong>Enter</strong> or <strong>P</strong> to play, <strong>S</strong> for substitutions, <strong>B</strong> for the box score, and <strong>H</strong> for a hint. Number keys select strategies, pitches, or alignments. When managing both teams, use plain numbers for offense and Shift + number for defense. The menu's <strong>Keyboard Cards</strong> page lists every key.</p>
        </div>
    </DialogContent>
    <DialogActions>
        <MudButton Color="Color.Primary" OnClick="@(() => MudDialog.Close())">Close</MudButton>
    </DialogActions>
</MudDialog>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] private IMudDialogInstance MudDialog { get; set; } = null!;
}
